- Each line should express only one image or one action.
- Avoid stacking multiple concepts in the same line.
- Each couplet must include at least one physical image, object, or scene.
- Ground abstract meaning in visible action, setting, sensory detail, or symbolic object.
- Draw concrete wording from the source text rather than generic uplift language.
- Prefer present-tense realis declarative lines.
- Prefer shared witness and communal voice when it fits the source.
- Prefer `we`, `our`, or collective experiential phrasing over `you` commands.
- Let the verse deepen or turn the song rather than restate the chorus.

Avoid:
- second-person address unless clearly required by the source
- direct instruction
- bare imperatives
- hypothetical or wishful framing
- future-tense promise language unless clearly source-supported
- negation-heavy phrasing unless clearly source-required
